kers child labor  and child soldiers in many ways this  anime takes me back to the Industrial  Revolution and the Marx  critique of capitalist societies and as  a student of politics and philosophy  these are very intriguing subjects for  me so yeah what really stands out to me  with this anime is that it's not just an  action mecha anime the action scenes are  great they're really entertaining  however there is a deeper core to this  anime a deeper social economical  background that is explored in a really  mature and unique way and if you haven't  seen it yet be warned from this point on  there will be some spoilers so if you  still need to finish the anime go do  that right now and then come right back  season one follows techadon the company  that the orphans have formed as they  struggle to make it on their own as a  company and face many many challenges  however even though they lose some  comrades along the way at the end of  season one we do see techadon become a  powerful force in the world of the anime  and while some anime would have ended  the journey right there iron blood and  orphans does not do that at the  beginning of season 2 we see the issue  of child soldiers rear its ugly head  again in an interesting way because  techadon which was made up of orphans or  child soldiers became so powerful and  successful other companies started using  and abusing more child soldiers and  following in techadons example I found  this very interesting because while we  know that the orphans attack Adan had  the best intentions they didn't have a  choice but to rebel and create their own  company their success actually hurt  other people because now more child  soldiers were being used because child  soldiers were proven to be so effective  with the example of techadon this is an  instance of the anime actually thinking  about the consequences the real-world  consequences of actions and this is  something that keeps coming up again and  again throughout the anime as techadon  becomes more powerful they also have  more responsibilities and more dangerous  rivals they become a target and they  have to deal with the consequences of  their actions both for themselves and  for others the anime handles these  issues maturely it is not merely a case  of the good guys defeating the bad guys  and then everything works out for  everyone the issues here are much more  complicated and to emphasize again how  the show handles socio-economic issues  in a really mature way I want to bring  up a  detail from episode 35 this detail might  seem small at first it might seem  insignificant but in my own personal  experience it is actually quite  significant in this episode Judah Leah  tells us that she established a school  for orphans and in this school both the  tuition and the lunch are free and lunch  is free because she doesn't want the  orphans to have to work in order to be  able to afford food and this free lunch  stuff is actually an important detail in  my own experience as a child I actually  lived through the civil wars in the  former Yugoslavia and my family and I  were refugees as a refugee I moved to a  new school where they had a yearly lunch  plan and basically you would pay a very  affordable yearly fee in order to get  lunch every day served to you along with  the rest of your classmates now I was  fortunate enough that my parents could  actually afford this yearly fee so I  would get lunch served along with my  classmates every day and then it would  be a social experience as well we would  talk about what lunch is being served  this day if we're being served the same  thing too often and all that kind of  elementary school preschool kind of  chatter and talk however because of the  difficult economic situation as a result  of the civil war there were always one  or two students in our classes who could  not afford even this small fee for lunch  I still remember feeling bad for these  students who could not afford the lunch  